  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "A whole liner vanished off South Africa with 211 aboard. July 1909. The Waratah, barely a year old, on only her second voyage.",
        "visual_query": "south african coastline rough seas",
        "visual_fallback": "rough seas"
      },
      {
        "narration": "She left Durban at quarter past eight in the evening. At dawn a freighter swapped lamp signals with her, then lost sight of her by mid-morning.",
        "visual_query": "vintage steamship ocean horizon",
        "visual_fallback": "steamship horizon"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Nobody ever saw her again. Royal Navy cruisers searched. One chartered ship covered fourteen thousand nautical miles. Not a lifeboat. Not a body.",
        "visual_query": "empty indian ocean open water",
        "visual_fallback": "open ocean"
      },
      {
        "narration": "In 1999 sonar seemed to find her at last. Divers reached that wreck in 2001 and it was a cargo ship, sunk by a U-boat decades later.",
        "visual_query": "shipwreck underwater sonar survey",
        "visual_fallback": "underwater shipwreck"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Passengers had complained she rolled badly. The leading theory is a top-heavy ship caught by one of the twenty-metre rogue waves that coast is known for.",
        "visual_query": "giant storm wave ocean swell",
        "visual_fallback": "storm waves"
      }
    ],
